{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I verified the implementation, spec, author's report, and reviewer's findings all directly. Everything matches. Now I can render an architect's ruling.\n\nRuling on the invariants:\n\n**1. Expiry contract and behavior — drain first, then scan for minimum-expiry id:**\n- Drain first: correct. Responses arriving on the deadline tick itself (within drain budget) clear the entry before the scan — this minimizes false losses. Under a flood that saturates the budget, false losses can occur — this was explicitly accepted in D3 (\"what a flood cannot delay is expiry. The cost of a false loss is redial, and it invalidates nothing\"). Tested in `flood-cannot-defer-expiry`.\n- Whole-channel loss via `channel-loss!`: architecturally correct. `give-up-after` is a liveness statement about the channel, not about individual requests (spec §1.3, docs §2.4). On an ordered, reliable channel, the fact that the minimum-id request has expired without a response means the peer is not responding. Responses for subsequent requests are either in transit (and will be lost anyway) or will never come. Reusing the existing `channel-loss!` path means no new loss semantics are added: `append-unknown` per abandoned append (honest reporting of effect-unknown, per 2.5 — append! is never resent, so its fate must be reported, not guessed), non-retriable `channel-gone` for cursor/next/resolve, `descriptor` still `ok` (name outlives the named), filed responses preserved. Non-retriable is right: retrying over a dead channel is meaningless, and redial is the caller's/driver's policy (S3a). Nothing is observable from yin beyond the channel-gone/transport-error it already handles — preserving the D3 \"no new head values\" stance. The `channel-expired` event before loss provides observability/diagnostics for distinguishing expiry from `end`. Upheld.\n\nOne nuance: abandonment of *unexpired* concurrent in-flight requests. Their appends are reported as append-unknown — honest. Their next/cursor are simply lost along with the channel, and re-requested after redial (idempotent, recomputable). No protocol violation: ids are asker-minted per channel, and a fresh dial mints fresh ids on a new link. Upheld.\n\n**2. Dial teardown:** Sound. Ownership rule (stance rule 5 / §2.4: \"whoever owns the connection closes it on loss\") — dial owns the ws handle, so dial closes it. Ordering within `dial-step!` (projection → link step → mirror → close) is correct: fresh deposits reach the link before the expiry scan. Close is guarded by `closable?` and `close!` is idempotent → repeated post-loss ticks are safe. The close flows through the host's `:ws/closed` deposit → projection closes the ring → subsequent operations see the end state. Tested. The 1-arity is preserved for existing consumers (connect.cljc, linker head) — zero-breakage by construction. Also, `establish!` steps the fresh link with the dial's last `now` before sending the attach probe — this behavior goes beyond the letter but is necessary to bound the establishment-stall on the dial path, and it was correctly approved by the S2b signoff note mechanism. Upheld.\n\n**3. Findings ruling:**\n- Finding 1 (`(step cd nil)` erases `:now` and throws on `(<= deadline nil)` if a deadline exists): verified in code (line 1113 unconditionally swaps in `:now now`, line 1119 some-> threads deadline into `(<= deadline now)` — nil now + present deadline throws on all three hosts). Unreachable via `dial-step!` (line 608 `some? now` guard), but `:step` is public API on `links`. Accept as a non-blocking P3 follow-up: in S3a, add a one-line guard (skip recording and skip scan when now is nil — matches the documented \"nothing expires without a stepped now\". Prefer this over throwing, since 1-arity dial-step! delegates nil by design). Should be fixed before external composition consumes `links` `:step` directly.\n- Finding 2 (requests that pre-existed the first timed step don't get a deadline, no backfill): conforms to the letter of the spec (\"nothing is stamped before a step with now\"). Ruling: document the driver's contract — the driver must step with `now` before the first attach/resolve. The dial path already enforces this via `establish!`. Backfill on the first timed step is rejected for S2c: stamping an old request with a \"now + give-up-after\" deadline at the first timed step conflates the driver's start time with the request's send time, and silently changing the deadline semantics is a spec amendment, not a follow-up. If S3a finds that real drivers cannot guarantee step-before-attach, revisit backfill as a spec amendment at that point. Accepted as an S3a follow-up, with a docs note in the S3a slice.\n- Findings 3–5 (P4 hygiene: double computation of new-deadline in send-named!, `:pending` surviving loss, no named-expiry test): non-blocking, tracked as S3a hygiene. Note finding 4 — pending entries surviving channel-loss! is indeed inert (drain! returns early when gone. Send paths guard on channel-gone? — wait, does retry-pending! run inside drain! before the gone check? No: line 747 of drain! `when-not channel-gone?` wraps everything including retry-pending!. And send-request! is called from operations… refl operations check gone? first. OK, inert).\n\n**4. Verdict:** Conditional SIGN-OFF GRANTED: rerun the test lane before landing (the reviewer couldn't run tests; the author reported 83/609 green — the signoff should require the committer to rerun), land as a single commit with the proposed message, track findings 1, 2, 5 as S3a follow-ups.\n\nNow — I'm in plan mode. I can't write to collab/. I need to write the plan file to ~/.commandcode/plans/, include the full signoff report, and set up the plan so that on approval the report is written to collab/1791360593148-architect-stream-s2c-signoff.qwen3.8-max.findings.md. Note that the task header says model qwen/qwen3.8-max, but I'm Command Code — the filename says qwen3.8-max, so I'll keep the requested filename.\n\nAlso should double-check the test lane status: can't run tests (plan mode, and shell is blocked). The signoff will require a rerun before commit. The task says \"complete, verified, reviewer-accepted diff\" and cites local verification of 83 tests/609 assertions. Accept the cited verification and require the committer to rerun.\n\nLet me also quickly verify one detail the reviewer flagged: finding 1's line `(some-> (:deadline e) (<= now))` — this is `(<= deadline now)`. If now is nil → throws. Correct.\n\nAlso verify the pending lookup order in `send-request!`: lines 462-466 — compute deadline via pending lookup before the cond. On retry of a retained probe (via retry-pending! → send-request!), the pending entry still exists so it keeps its original deadline. On acceptance, register! with that deadline. Verified — never re-stamped.\n\n`count-ask!` (484-497): only touches `:asks`, resends the same req. Verified.\n\nAnother architectural check: `link-step!`'s return `(first overdue)` — overdue is a [id e] pair, so `:expired` is id. Good. Also `:step` on a reached but un-attached channel creates the link via link-for! — harmless, pre-records now.\n\nSort-by key over concat of outstanding and pending: could id collide between outstanding and pending? Pending entries are removed from outstanding… retained probes are in pending only until accepted. Ids are unique per link (mint-id!). But could an id be in both maps? register! adds to outstanding and dissocs from pending in the same cond branch (lines 469-471: after register!, swap dissocs pending — two separate swaps. Momentarily in both, but single-threaded driver discipline). No issue.\n\nOK let me write the plan file with the full signoff report. The plan file path: ~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2c-architect-signoff.md. Plan: the sole action is to write the findings file to collab/. I'll include the full report contents in the plan so implementation is a single write.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I've verified everything firsthand: the `link-step!` implementation (remote.cljc:1105–1129), deadline stamping across all send paths (`send-request!` pending-lookup-first at 462–466, `refl-append` 927, `send-named!` 990–993"}]}}
