{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_end","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I verified the implementation, spec, author's report, and reviewer's findings all directly. Everything matches. Now I can render an architect's ruling.\n\nRuling on the invariants:\n\n**1. Expiry contract and behavior — drain first, then scan for minimum-expiry id:**\n- Drain first: correct. Responses arriving on the deadline tick itself (within drain budget) clear the entry before the scan — this minimizes false losses. Under a flood that saturates the budget, false losses can occur — this was explicitly accepted in D3 (\"what a flood cannot delay is expiry. The cost of a false loss is redial, and it invalidates nothing\"). Tested in `flood-cannot-defer-expiry`.\n- Whole-channel loss via `channel-loss!`: architecturally correct. `give-up-after` is a liveness statement about the channel, not about individual requests (spec §1.3, docs §2.4). On an ordered, reliable channel, the fact that the minimum-id request has expired without a response means the peer is not responding. Responses for subsequent requests are either in transit (and will be lost anyway) or will never come. Reusing the existing `channel-loss!` path means no new loss semantics are added: `append-unknown` per abandoned append (honest reporting of effect-unknown, per 2.5 — append! is never resent, so its fate must be reported, not guessed), non-retriable `channel-gone` for cursor/next/resolve, `descriptor` still `ok` (name outlives the named), filed responses preserved. Non-retriable is right: retrying over a dead channel is meaningless, and redial is the caller's/driver's policy (S3a). Nothing is observable from yin beyond the channel-gone/transport-error it already handles — preserving the D3 \"no new head values\" stance. The `channel-expired` event before loss provides observability/diagnostics for distinguishing expiry from `end`. Upheld.\n\nOne nuance: abandonment of *unexpired* concurrent in-flight requests. Their appends are reported as append-unknown — honest. Their next/cursor are simply lost along with the channel, and re-requested after redial (idempotent, recomputable). No protocol violation: ids are asker-minted per channel, and a fresh dial mints fresh ids on a new link. Upheld.\n\n**2. Dial teardown:** Sound. Ownership rule (stance rule 5 / §2.4: \"whoever owns the connection closes it on loss\") — dial owns the ws handle, so dial closes it. Ordering within `dial-step!` (projection → link step → mirror → close) is correct: fresh deposits reach the link before the expiry scan. Close is guarded by `closable?` and `close!` is idempotent → repeated post-loss ticks are safe. The close flows through the host's `:ws/closed` deposit → projection closes the ring → subsequent operations see the end state. Tested. The 1-arity is preserved for existing consumers (connect.cljc, linker head) — zero-breakage by construction. Also, `establish!` steps the fresh link with the dial's last `now` before sending the attach probe — this behavior goes beyond the letter but is necessary to bound the establishment-stall on the dial path, and it was correctly approved by the S2b signoff note mechanism. Upheld.\n\n**3. Findings ruling:**\n- Finding 1 (`(step cd nil)` erases `:now` and throws on `(<= deadline nil)` if a deadline exists): verified in code (line 1113 unconditionally swaps in `:now now`, line 1119 some-> threads deadline into `(<= deadline now)` — nil now + present deadline throws on all three hosts). Unreachable via `dial-step!` (line 608 `some? now` guard), but `:step` is public API on `links`. Accept as a non-blocking P3 follow-up: in S3a, add a one-line guard (skip recording and skip scan when now is nil — matches the documented \"nothing expires without a stepped now\". Prefer this over throwing, since 1-arity dial-step! delegates nil by design). Should be fixed before external composition consumes `links` `:step` directly.\n- Finding 2 (requests that pre-existed the first timed step don't get a deadline, no backfill): conforms to the letter of the spec (\"nothing is stamped before a step with now\"). Ruling: document the driver's contract — the driver must step with `now` before the first attach/resolve. The dial path already enforces this via `establish!`. Backfill on the first timed step is rejected for S2c: stamping an old request with a \"now + give-up-after\" deadline at the first timed step conflates the driver's start time with the request's send time, and silently changing the deadline semantics is a spec amendment, not a follow-up. If S3a finds that real drivers cannot guarantee step-before-attach, revisit backfill as a spec amendment at that point. Accepted as an S3a follow-up, with a docs note in the S3a slice.\n- Findings 3–5 (P4 hygiene: double computation of new-deadline in send-named!, `:pending` surviving loss, no named-expiry test): non-blocking, tracked as S3a hygiene. Note finding 4 — pending entries surviving channel-loss! is indeed inert (drain! returns early when gone. Send paths guard on channel-gone? — wait, does retry-pending! run inside drain! before the gone check? No: line 747 of drain! `when-not channel-gone?` wraps everything including retry-pending!. And send-request! is called from operations… refl operations check gone? first. OK, inert).\n\n**4. Verdict:** Conditional SIGN-OFF GRANTED: rerun the test lane before landing (the reviewer couldn't run tests; the author reported 83/609 green — the signoff should require the committer to rerun), land as a single commit with the proposed message, track findings 1, 2, 5 as S3a follow-ups.\n\nNow — I'm in plan mode. I can't write to collab/. I need to write the plan file to ~/.commandcode/plans/, include the full signoff report, and set up the plan so that on approval the report is written to collab/1791360593148-architect-stream-s2c-signoff.qwen3.8-max.findings.md. Note that the task header says model qwen/qwen3.8-max, but I'm Command Code — the filename says qwen3.8-max, so I'll keep the requested filename.\n\nAlso should double-check the test lane status: can't run tests (plan mode, and shell is blocked). The signoff will require a rerun before commit. The task says \"complete, verified, reviewer-accepted diff\" and cites local verification of 83 tests/609 assertions. Accept the cited verification and require the committer to rerun.\n\nLet me also quickly verify one detail the reviewer flagged: finding 1's line `(some-> (:deadline e) (<= now))` — this is `(<= deadline now)`. If now is nil → throws. Correct.\n\nAlso verify the pending lookup order in `send-request!`: lines 462-466 — compute deadline via pending lookup before the cond. On retry of a retained probe (via retry-pending! → send-request!), the pending entry still exists so it keeps its original deadline. On acceptance, register! with that deadline. Verified — never re-stamped.\n\n`count-ask!` (484-497): only touches `:asks`, resends the same req. Verified.\n\nAnother architectural check: `link-step!`'s return `(first overdue)` — overdue is a [id e] pair, so `:expired` is id. Good. Also `:step` on a reached but un-attached channel creates the link via link-for! — harmless, pre-records now.\n\nSort-by key over concat of outstanding and pending: could id collide between outstanding and pending? Pending entries are removed from outstanding… retained probes are in pending only until accepted. Ids are unique per link (mint-id!). But could an id be in both maps? register! adds to outstanding and dissocs from pending in the same cond branch (lines 469-471: after register!, swap dissocs pending — two separate swaps. Momentarily in both, but single-threaded driver discipline). No issue.\n\nOK let me write the plan file with the full signoff report. The plan file path: ~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2c-architect-signoff.md. Plan: the sole action is to write the findings file to collab/. I'll include the full report contents in the plan so implementation is a single write.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I've verified everything firsthand: the `link-step!` implementation (remote.cljc:1105–1129), deadline stamping across all send paths (`send-request!` pending-lookup-first at 462–466, `refl-append` 927, `send-named!` 990–993, `count-ask!` never touching `:deadline`), `channel-loss!` (698–711), `dial-step!` multi-arity with the guarded `some? now` (600–619), `establish!`'s pre-probe step (513–514), and the doc amendments in §2.4/§2.5/§3.0. Both reviewer findings 1 and 2 reproduce exactly as described in the code.\n\nNow I'll write the sign-off report into the plan file — since I'm in plan mode, the actual write to `collab/` happens once the plan is approved.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_2a1ddfd31f644725862e9c56","name":"write_file","input":{"file_path":"~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2c-architect-signoff.md","content":"# Plan: Save S2c Architect Sign-Off Findings\n\n## Context\nArchitect review of Slice S2c (request liveness & channel expiry) on branch `stream-crossmachine-s2` @ `91ab84f7` is complete. The review was performed read-only and firsthand against the working tree. The only implementation action is saving the findings report — blocked in plan mode.\n\n## Action (single step)\nWrite the report below verbatim to:\n`collab/1791360593148-architect-stream-s2c-signoff.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`\n\nNo source, test, or doc file changes. No commits (the committer re-runs test lanes and lands the commit per the verdict).\n\n## Verification\n- File exists at the target path with the full report content.\n- `git status` shows only the new untracked `collab/` file beyond the existing S2c working-tree changes.\n\n---\n\n## Report content to save\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-07 08:10:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 15:10:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: cmd (qwen/qwen3.8-max)\n\n# Lead System Architect: Slice S2c sign-off — request liveness and channel expiry\n\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`, branch `stream-crossmachine-s2`, base `91ab84f7`, uncommitted S2c diff.\n\nRead firsthand for this sign-off: `remote.cljc` (`new-deadline`, `register!`, `send-request!`, `count-ask!`, `channel-loss!`, `drain!`, `refl-append`, `send-named!`, `link-step!`, `links`), `ws_project.cljc` (`dial` policy select-keys, `establish!`, `dial-step!` both arities), `dao.stream.remote.md` §2.4 Channel loss + Expiry, §2.5, §3.0 Request-liveness bullet, the S2 architectural spec (`collab/1791353752491-...claude-fable-5-1.findings.md` §0 rule 5, §1.2–§1.3, §2, §3, §4 S2c row), the author report (`collab/1791358000000-...claude-opus-5-5.findings.md`) and the independent review (`collab/1791359934597-...qwen3.8-max.findings.md`). Shell execution was blocked in this session; the cited local verification (83 tests / 609 assertions / 0 failures, cljstyle and kondo clean) is taken from the author report and the dispatch evidence, and is made a landing condition below.\n\n## 1. Expiry contract and behavior\n\n**Ruling: upheld.**\n\n**Drain-then-scan ordering.** `link-step!` records `:now`, runs the budgeted `drain!`, then scans `(concat outstanding pending)` for the least id with `:deadline <= now` (remote.cljc:1113–1121). Draining first is the correct ordering: an answer arriving in the deadline's own tick clears its entry before the scan, minimizing false loss without weakening the bound. The scan is independent of the drain budget, so a flood saturating the budget cannot defer expiry — it can only cause a false loss, which is exactly the D3 trade-off the mob consensus accepted and `flood-cannot-defer-expiry` demonstrates. No valid concurrent answer is dropped by the ordering itself: answers already drained are filed before the scan runs.\n\n**Whole-channel loss on one expired request.** Architecturally correct and the only response consistent with the design stance. `give-up-after` is a liveness statement about the *channel*, not the request (spec §1.3; doc §2.4): on an ordered reliable channel, the least-id request unanswered past its deadline means the peer is not serving — later in-flight requests are either equally unserved or their answers were lost with the channel. Reusing the existing `channel-loss!` path (remote.cljc:698–711) adds no new loss semantics:\n\n- `append-unknown` per abandoned append is the honest 2.5 report: `append!` is never re-sent, so an unanswered append's effect must be reported unknown, never guessed. Abandoning *unexpired* concurrent appends reports them the same way — correct, since the channel that would carry their answers is declared dead.\n- Non-retryable `channel-gone` for `cursor`/`next`/`resolve` is right: a retry over a dead channel is meaningless; redial is the caller's/driver's policy (S3a). `descriptor` still answering `ok` preserves \"a name outlives what it named\".\n- Idempotent requests lost with the channel are recomputable after redial; ids are asker-minted per link, so a fresh dial mints fresh ids — no stale-id hazard.\n- Yin observes only the `channel-gone`/`transport-error` it already handles; the new `channel-expired` event goes to the composition's event writer for observability, distinguishing expiry from `end`. No new head value (D3 preserved).\n\nThe `channel-gone?` guard on the scan (remote.cljc:1116) prevents a double report when an `end`-loss and an expiry coincide in one step. Verified.\n\n## 2. Dial teardown\n\n**Ruling: sound and idempotent.**\n\nOwnership follows the stance rule: whatever owns the connection closes it on loss, and the dial owns the ws handle. `dial-step!`'s 2-arity orders projection → link step → mirror → close (ws_project.cljc:600–619): the projection runs before the link step so freshly deposited answers reach the link before the expiry scan; the close is guarded by `(:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? stepped)` and `(stream/closable? handle)`, and `close!` is idempotent — repeated post-loss ticks are safe (a nil `stepped` is falsy; after loss, `link-step!` still answers `channel-gone? true` with `expired nil`, and `closable?` is false once closed). The teardown then flows through the host's `:ws/closed` deposit → projection closes the ring → the composition observes ordinary closed state. Tested end-to-end in `dial-step-with-now-closes-the-handle-on-expiry`.\n\nThe 1-arity delegation preserves every existing consumer (`yin/repl/connect.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc`) unchanged — zero-breakage by construction, per the S2 spec's nil-default discipline.\n\n`establish!` stepping the new link at the dial's recorded `now` before the attach probe is sent (ws_project.cljc:513–514) is the one beyond-the-letter behavior. **Ratified.** Without it, the probe `dial-attach!` sends immediately would be unstamped and the establishment stall Expiry exists to bound (dao.stream.ws.md Deferred, first bullet; spec §1.3's deliberate kept-probe deviation) would go unbounded on the dial path. The mechanism is minimal: `:step` creates the link via `link-for!` and pre-records `:now`, so the later `:attach` reuses the same link. A clock-less dial is untouched.\n\n## 3. Findings ruling\n\n**Finding 1 (P3, `(step cd nil)`).** Confirmed in code: `link-step!` unconditionally records `:now nil` (erasing a previously stepped `now`) and the scan's `(some-> (:deadline e) (<= now))` throws on `(<= deadline nil)` once any entry carries a deadline — on all three hosts. Unreachable via `dial-step!` (guarded by `some? now` at ws_project.cljc:608), but `:step` is public API of `links`. **Ruling: acceptable for S2c; required fix before any composition outside ws-project consumes `links` `:step` directly — track as an S3a follow-up.** The guard should *skip* (not throw) on nil `now`: skipping matches the documented \"without a stepped `now`, nothing expires\" and keeps `:step` total; throwing would make `(step cd nil)` a hazard the 1-arity dial-step! delegates into by design. One line: record `:now` and run the scan only `(when (some? now) ...)`, still draining either way is optional — simplest is an early nil-`now` path that drains without scanning.\n\n**Finding 2 (P3, requests existing before the first timed step).** Confirmed: deadline stamping happens at first existence on the link; nothing backfills at the first timed step. This follows the spec's letter (\"before any step with `now`, nothing is stamped\"); the spec is silent on backfill. **Ruling: acceptable as an S3a follow-up, resolved by driver contract, not by backfill.** The S3a driver composition must step with `now` before first attach/resolve — the dial path already enforces this via `establish!`; the direct-`links` path must document it. Backfilling at the first timed step is rejected for now: stamping a long-standing request `now + give-up-after` at driver start conflates driver-start with send time, and changing deadline semantics is a spec amendment requiring its own review. If S3a finds a driver that genuinely cannot step-before-attach, revisit backfill as an explicit spec amendment then. Record the driver obligation in §2.4 Expiry during S3a.\n\n**Findings 3–5 (P4).** Double `new-deadline` computation in `send-named!` (pure; let-bind at S3a hygiene), `:pending` surviving `channel-loss!` (inert: `drain!` wraps everything including `retry-pending!` in `when-not channel-gone?`, remote.cljc:747; optional hygiene), and the missing named-request expiry test (add with finding 1's guard test in S3a). All non-blocking.\n\n## 4. Verdict\n\n**SIGN-OFF GRANTED** for staging and committing Slice S2c as\n`feat(dao.stream.remote): request deadlines, channel-expired and link step (cross-machine stream slice S2c)`,\nunder these landing conditions:\n\n1. The committer re-runs `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test` (expect 83 tests / 609 assertions / 0 failures / 0 errors), `cljstyle check` and `clj -M:kondo` on the four changed files, plus the full `bb test` three-host lane and kondo before the sub-slice commit, per `docs/agents/build-n-test.md` — the reviewer and this sign-off could not execute them in-session.\n2. S2d does not rebase over unlanded S2c assumptions: S2c lands alone as one commit.\n3. Tracked as S3a follow-ups (record in the S3a dispatch): finding 1's nil-`now` guard on `link-step!`; finding 2's driver-obligation doc note in §2.4; finding 5's named-request expiry test; findings 3–4 as hygiene.\n\nThe slice implements the S2 spec §1.2/§1.3 S2c rows, the §2 failure-table expiry rows, the §3 S2c test plan and the §4 doc amendments faithfully. Deadline discipline is verified on every path: stamped at first existence (accepted or kept), kept probes carry their original deadline (pending lookup precedes the cond in `send-request!`, so a retry never restamps), waiting probes unstamped until `admit-probe!`, `count-ask!` never moves a deadline, answers clear entries deadline-included. The reflection still reads no clock; `now` is the driver's alone. Expiry is whole-channel loss through the existing path, and the connection owner tears down. D1/D2/D3, S0 and S1 invariants are preserved.\n\n## 5. Verification of this pass\n\nRead-only. No source, test, doc or collab file was modified by the review itself; shell execution was blocked in-session, so no tests were run and no `git diff` was executed — verification was against the full working-tree files, cross-checked line by line against the author report and the independent review, whose finding locations all reproduce.\n"}}]}}
